@page "/privacy"

@* What the app stores and why, as the code does it. Keep this page in step when a feature starts storing something
   new or sending data somewhere new, and update LastUpdated. What it was checked against, and what a lawyer still has
   to confirm for the place the operator is established, is in docs/legal-review.md. *@

<PageTitle>Privacy · @Brand.AppName</PageTitle>

<article class="legal">
    <h1>Privacy policy</h1>
    <p class="muted">Last updated @LastUpdated</p>

    <p>
        This policy explains what @Brand.AppName keeps about you, why, who else sees any of it, how long we keep it, and
        how you can get a copy or have it deleted.
    </p>

    <h2>Who we are</h2>
    <p>
        @if (Brand.Operator != Brand.AppName)
        {
            <span>@Brand.AppName is run by @Brand.Operator ("we")</span>
            @if (Brand.CompanyNumber is { } number)
            {
                <span>, @number</span>
            }
            @if (Brand.OperatorAddress is { } address)
            {
                <span>, of @address</span>
            }
            <span>. </span>
        }
        We decide what your data is used for, which makes us the "controller" under European law and the "responsible
        party" under South Africa's.
        @if (Brand.ContactEmail is { } email)
        {
            <span> Anything about your data, including the rights below, goes to <a href="mailto:@email">@email</a>,
                which reaches the person responsible for data protection here.</span>
        }
    </p>

    <h2>The short version</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>We keep what you tell the app (your shows, the episodes you've watched, your ratings and posts) so it can work
            for you. We don't sell it, and there are no advertising or analytics trackers.</li>
        <li>Figures about our users that others can see, such as how many people finished a season, need at least five
            people behind them, so they can't point to you.</li>
        <li>You can download everything we hold about you, and delete your account, in Settings at any time.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What we keep</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Your account:</strong> your username, email address and password (held by our sign-in service, with the
            password stored only as a hash), and your profile: display name, picture address, time zone, region and
            language.</li>
        <li><strong>Your viewing:</strong> the shows you follow and their status, the episodes you mark as watched and
            when, your lists, your ratings of shows and episodes, your episode duels and your predictions.</li>
        <li><strong>What you write for others:</strong> comments, tips and community insights, suggested corrections,
            flags on other people's posts, and the helpful votes you give.</li>
        <li><strong>People:</strong> who you follow, who follows you, follow requests, the people you've blocked, and your
            privacy settings.</li>
        <li><strong>Notifications:</strong> your notification preferences, your inbox, and the devices you've allowed to
            receive push notifications.</li>
        <li><strong>Plans and payments:</strong> your plan, your family plan's members, your subscription (plan, price,
            period, whether it renews) and a receipt for each payment. Your card details go to the payment provider and
            never reach us.</li>
        <li><strong>History you import:</strong> the file is read on your device; only the shows, episodes and dates it
            contains are sent to us, and account files in an export archive are never opened.</li>
        <li><strong>Streaming services</strong> you tell us you pay for, to show what they cost you per hour watched.</li>
        <li><strong>Technical records:</strong> when you use the app our servers see your device's network (IP) address
            and what was asked for. We use the address to limit how fast any one visitor can make requests, and it can
            appear in the server logs we use to find faults. We don't build a profile from it.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        We don't ask for, and the app has no place for, sensitive information such as your health, beliefs or
        government identity numbers. Please don't put any in a comment or a profile.
    </p>

    <h2>What stays on your device</h2>
    <p>
        Some settings never leave the device you set them on: the theme, the spoiler shield, your birth year (for Your TV
        life), your birthday (for Birthdays) and your Guess the show record. The web app keeps them in your browser's
        storage, the phone app in its own preferences.
    </p>

    <h2>Why we use it, and what allows us to</h2>
    <div class="table-scroll">
        <table class="table">
            <thead><tr><th>What for</th><th>What allows it</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td>Running the service you signed up for: your account, tracking, calendars, recommendations,
                        insights, the social features, and a paid plan if you buy one</td>
                    <td>Our agreement with you (the <a href="terms">terms</a>)</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Taking payments and keeping the records tax and accounting law requires</td>
                    <td>Our agreement with you, and legal duties we have</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Keeping the service safe and working: rate limits, moderation of posts, logs for finding faults,
                        and backups</td>
                    <td>Our legitimate interest in a service that is secure and reliable, which doesn't override yours</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Figures about many users together, and counts of clicks on streaming links</td>
                    <td>Our legitimate interest in understanding and funding the service; neither identifies you</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Push notifications, emails about your shows and the weekly digest</td>
                    <td>Your choice: push needs the permission your device asks you for, emails and the digest are off
                        until you turn them on, and you can turn any of them off again in Settings</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
    <p>
        Recommendations, forecasts and insights are worked out by software from the episodes and ratings of shows, and
        from your own viewing for the ones about you. They decide nothing about you that has a legal or similarly
        serious effect. Where an insight is about other users, we show it only with five or more people or ratings
        behind it.
    </p>

    <h2>What others can see</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>People who follow you see your activity (episodes watched, shows followed, finished and rated) unless you
            turn off "share my activity". A private profile shows only your name to anyone you haven't approved.</li>
        <li>Your comments and other posts are public to signed-in users, under your display name. Episode titles are never
            shown in the activity feed, only episode numbers.</li>
        <li>A list you share is visible on your profile; a list link can be opened by anyone who has it, until you turn
            the link off.</li>
        <li>Family plan members see the owner's name and each other's names, nothing more.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Who else receives data</h2>
    <p>We don't sell your data or give it to advertisers. These are the only others involved:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Our hosting provider</strong> runs the servers the app and its databases are on, and any storage
            our backups are copied to. It handles data only on our instructions.</li>
        <li><strong>Our payment provider</strong> (Stripe) handles checkout and billing, including your card and billing
            details, under its own privacy policy. We tell it which plan you're buying; it tells us when a payment
            succeeds.</li>
        <li><strong>Our email delivery provider</strong> receives your email address and the message when we send you
            one, to deliver it.</li>
        <li><strong>Push services:</strong> a push notification travels through the service your browser or phone uses
            for them (run by Apple, Google or Mozilla, for example), which receives the notification and an address for
            your device.</li>
        <li><strong>Streaming services</strong> you open from a show page: the link goes through us so we can count clicks
            per service, without recording who clicked. Some links carry an affiliate code, so the service may pay us a
            commission on a sign-up.</li>
        <li><strong>Show pictures</strong> are loaded by your browser or phone from @Brand.Catalog.ImagesFrom, which therefore sees your
            device's network address, as any website you visit does.</li>
        <li>Our show data comes from @(Brand.Catalog.DataFrom). We fetch it ourselves, and send them nothing about
            you; when you import a history, the titles of shows we don't yet have are looked up there, without your
            name.</li>
        <li>We may disclose data where the law requires it, or to someone who takes over the service, who would be bound
            by this policy.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Where your data is kept</h2>
    <p>
        Your data is kept on servers our hosting provider runs, and some of the providers above are in other countries,
        so it may be handled outside the country you live in. Where the law asks for it, we rely on a safeguard it
        recognises for that, such as an agreement with the provider on standard terms approved for the purpose, or a
        decision that the other country protects data adequately.
    </p>

    <h2>Cookies</h2>
    <p>
        The web app sets a cookie to keep you signed in and one that protects the forms you send, and the sign-in service
        sets its own while you sign in. All are needed for the app to work, so there is no cookie banner to answer.
        There are no advertising or analytics cookies.
    </p>

    <h2>How long we keep it</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>Your account and what you've added stay until you delete them or your account.</li>
        <li>Notifications are removed 90 days after you've read them, and after a year at the latest.</li>
        <li>You stay signed in on a device for up to seven days after you last used it there.</li>
        <li>Payment receipts are kept while your account exists; the payment provider keeps its own records for as long
            as the law requires.</li>
        <li>Backups of our databases are kept for about two weeks. Something you delete, or your whole account, is gone
            from the app at once and from the last backup that held it once that backup expires.</li>
        <li>Server logs are kept for a limited time, until newer ones replace them.</li>
        <li>Counts of clicks on streaming links carry no information about who clicked.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How we protect it</h2>
    <p>
        Everything between your device and us travels encrypted (HTTPS). Passwords are stored only as hashes, by a
        separate sign-in service. The databases can't be reached from the internet, and only the people who run the
        service can reach the servers. No system is perfectly safe: if a breach puts your data at risk, we'll tell you
        and the data protection authority, as the law requires.
    </p>

    <h2>Your rights</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>A copy of your data:</strong> Settings → Export my data gives you everything every part of the app
            holds about you, as a file you can take elsewhere.</li>
        <li><strong>Deleting your account:</strong> Settings → Delete account removes your account and everything you've
            added, from every part of the app, and stops any subscription at once. Posts you wrote go with it.</li>
        <li><strong>Correcting it:</strong> you can change your profile, ratings, lists and posts at any time.</li>
        <li><strong>Saying no:</strong> you can turn off notifications, emails and activity sharing in Settings, and
            object to, or ask us to limit, anything we do on the strength of a legitimate interest.</li>
        <li>
            These rights come from laws such as the GDPR in Europe and the UK, and POPIA in South Africa; which apply
            depends on where you live.
            @if (Brand.ContactEmail is { } rights)
            {
                <span> Write to <a href="mailto:@rights">@rights</a> for anything Settings doesn't cover; we answer
                    within a month.</span>
            }
        </li>
        <li><strong>Complaining:</strong> if you think we've handled your data wrongly, please tell us first. You may also
            complain to the data protection authority where you live: in the EU, your national authority; in the UK,
            the Information Commissioner's Office; in South Africa, the Information Regulator.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Children</h2>
    <p>
        @Brand.AppName isn't meant for children under 13, or under the age at which you can agree to this yourself where
        you live (16 in parts of Europe, 18 in South Africa without a parent's or guardian's consent). We don't knowingly
        keep their data, and delete it when we learn of it.
    </p>

    <h2>Changes</h2>
    <p>
        When we change this policy we'll update the date above, and tell you in the app before a change that affects
        what we do with data you've already given us.
    </p>

    <p class="muted"><a href="terms">Terms of service</a></p>
</article>

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// When the policy and the terms last changed; update it with any change to either text.
    /// </summary>
    internal const string LastUpdated = "14 November 2026";
}
